## 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#createagent)
`createAgent`
In v1, the react agent prebuilt is now in the langchain package. The table below outlines what functionality has changed:  
| Section  | What changed  |  
| --- | --- |  
| [Import path](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#import-path)  | Package moved from `@langchain/langgraph/prebuilts` to `langchain`  |  
| [Prompts](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#prompts)  | Parameter renamed to `systemPrompt`, dynamic prompts use middleware  |  
| [Pre-model hook](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#pre-model-hook)  | Replaced by middleware with `beforeModel` method  |  
| [Post-model hook](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#post-model-hook)  | Replaced by middleware with `afterModel` method  |  
| [Custom state](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#custom-state)  | Defined in middleware, zod objects only  |  
| [Model](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#model)  | Dynamic selection via middleware, pre-bound models not supported  |  
| [Tools](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#tools)  | Tool error handling moved to middleware with `wrapToolCall`  |  
| [Structured output](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#structured-output)  | prompted output removed, use `toolStrategy`/`providerStrategy`  |  
| [Streaming node name](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#streaming-node-name-rename)  | Node name changed from `"agent"` to `"model"`  |  
| [Runtime context](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#runtime-context)  |  `context` property instead of `config.configurable`  |  
| [Namespace](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#simplified-package)  | Streamlined to focus on agent building blocks, legacy code moved to `@langchain/classic`  |  
### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#import-path)
Import path
The import path for the react agent prebuilt has changed from `@langchain/langgraph/prebuilts` to `langchain`. The name of the function has changed from `createReactAgent` to `createAgent`:

```
import { createReactAgent } from "@langchain/langgraph/prebuilts";
import { createAgent } from "langchain";

```

### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#prompts)
Prompts
#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#static-prompt-rename)
Static prompt rename
The `prompt` parameter has been renamed to `systemPrompt`:
v1 (new)
v0 (old)

```
import { createAgent } from "langchain";

agent = createAgent({
  model,
  tools,
  systemPrompt: "You are a helpful assistant.",
});

```

#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#systemmessage)
`SystemMessage`
If using `SystemMessage` objects in the system prompt, the string content is now used directly:
v1 (new)
v0 (old)

```
import { SystemMessage, createAgent } from "langchain";

const agent = createAgent({
  model,
  tools,
  systemPrompt: "You are a helpful assistant.",
});

```

#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#dynamic-prompts)
Dynamic prompts
Dynamic prompts are a core context engineering pattern—they adapt what you tell the model based on the current conversation state. To do this, use `dynamicSystemPromptMiddleware`:
v1 (new)
v0 (old)

```
import { createAgent, dynamicSystemPromptMiddleware } from "langchain";
import * as z from "zod";

const contextSchema = z.object({
  userRole: z.enum(["expert", "beginner"]).default("beginner"),
});

const userRolePrompt = dynamicSystemPromptMiddleware<z.infer<typeof contextSchema>>( 
    (_state, runtime) => {
        const userRole = runtime.context.userRole;
        const basePrompt = "You are a helpful assistant.";

        if (userRole === "expert") {
            return `${basePrompt} Provide detailed technical responses.`;
        } else if (userRole === "beginner") {
            return `${basePrompt} Explain concepts simply and avoid jargon.`;
        }
        return basePrompt;
    }
);

const agent = createAgent({
  model,
  tools,
  middleware: [userRolePrompt],
  contextSchema,
});

await agent.invoke(
  {
    messages: [new HumanMessage("Explain async programming")],
  },
  {
    context: {
      userRole: "expert",
    },
  }
);

```

### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#pre-model-hook)
Pre-model hook
Pre-model hooks are now implemented as middleware with the `beforeModel` method. This pattern is more extensible—you can define multiple middlewares to run before the model is called and reuse them across agents. Common use cases include:
  * Summarizing conversation history
  * Trimming messages
  * Input guardrails, like PII redaction

v1 includes built-in summarization middleware:
v1 (new)
v0 (old)

```
import { createAgent, summarizationMiddleware } from "langchain";

const agent = createAgent({
  model: "claude-sonnet-4-6",
  tools,
  middleware: [
    summarizationMiddleware({
      model: "claude-sonnet-4-6",
      trigger: { tokens: 1000 },
    }),
  ],
});

```

### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#post-model-hook)
Post-model hook
Post-model hooks are now implemented as middleware with the `afterModel` method. This lets you compose multiple handlers after the model responds. Common use cases include:
  * Human-in-the-loop approval
  * Output guardrails

v1 includes a built-in human-in-the-loop middleware:
v1 (new)
v0 (old)

```
import { createAgent, humanInTheLoopMiddleware } from "langchain";

const agent = createAgent({
  model: "claude-sonnet-4-6",
  tools: [readEmail, sendEmail],
  middleware: [
    humanInTheLoopMiddleware({
      interruptOn: {
        sendEmail: { allowedDecisions: ["approve", "edit", "reject"] },
      },
    }),
  ],
});

```

### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#custom-state)
Custom state
Custom state is now defined in middleware using the `stateSchema` property. Use Zod to declare additional state fields that are carried through the agent run.
v1 (new)
v0 (old)

```
import * as z from "zod";
import { createAgent, createMiddleware, tool } from "langchain";

const UserState = z.object({
  userName: z.string(),
});

const userState = createMiddleware({
  name: "UserState",
  stateSchema: UserState,
  beforeModel: (state) => {
    // Access custom state properties
    const name = state.userName;
    // Optionally modify messages/system prompt based on state
    return;
  },
});

const greet = tool(
  async () => {
    return "Hello!";
  },
  {
    name: "greet",
    description: "Greet the user",
    schema: z.object({}),
  }
);

const agent = createAgent({
  model: "claude-sonnet-4-6",
  tools: [greet],
  middleware: [userState],
});

await agent.invoke({
  messages: [{ role: "user", content: "Hi" }],
  userName: "Ada",
});

```

### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#model)
Model
Dynamic model selection now happens via middleware. Use `wrapModelCall` to swap models (and tools) based on state or runtime context. In `createReactAgent`, this was done via a function passed to the `model` parameter. This functionality has been ported to the middleware interface in v1.
#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#dynamic-model-selection)
Dynamic model selection
v1 (new)
v0 (old)

```
import { createAgent, createMiddleware } from "langchain";

const dynamicModel = createMiddleware({
  name: "DynamicModel",
  wrapModelCall: (request, handler) => {
    const messageCount = request.state.messages.length;
    const model = messageCount > 10 ? "openai:gpt-5" : "openai:gpt-5-nano";
    return handler({ ...request, model });
  },
});

const agent = createAgent({
  model: "gpt-5-nano",
  tools,
  middleware: [dynamicModel],
});

```

#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#pre-bound-models)
Pre-bound models
To better support structured output, `createAgent` should receive a plain model (string or instance) and a separate `tools` list. Avoid passing models pre-bound with tools when using structured output.

```
// No longer supported
// const modelWithTools = new ChatOpenAI({ model: "gpt-4.1-mini" }).bindTools([someTool]);
// const agent = createAgent({ model: modelWithTools, tools: [] });

// Use instead
const agent = createAgent({ model: "gpt-4.1-mini", tools: [someTool] });

```

### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#tools)
Tools
The `tools` argument to `createAgent` accepts:
  * Functions created with `tool`
  * LangChain tool instances
  * Objects that represent built-in provider tools


#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#handling-tool-errors)
Handling tool errors
You can now configure the handling of tool errors with middleware implementing the `wrapToolCall` method.
v1 (new)
v0 (old)

```
import { createAgent, createMiddleware, ToolMessage } from "langchain";

const handleToolErrors = createMiddleware({
  name: "HandleToolErrors",
  wrapToolCall: async (request, handler) => {
    try {
      return await handler(request);
    } catch (error) {
      // Only handle errors that occur during tool execution due to invalid inputs
      // that pass schema validation but fail at runtime (e.g., invalid SQL syntax).
      // Do NOT handle:
      // - Network failures (use tool retry middleware instead)
      // - Incorrect tool implementation errors (should bubble up)
      // - Schema mismatch errors (already auto-handled by the framework)
      //
      // Return a custom error message to the model
      return new ToolMessage({
        content: `Tool error: Please check your input and try again. (${error})`,
        tool_call_id: request.toolCall.id!,
      });
    }
  },
});

const agent = createAgent({
  model: "claude-sonnet-4-6",
  tools: [checkWeather, searchWeb],
  middleware: [handleToolErrors],
});

```

### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#structured-output)
Structured output
#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#node-changes)
Node changes
Structured output used to be generated in a separate node from the main agent. This is no longer the case. Structured output is generated in the main loop (no extra LLM call), reducing cost and latency.
#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#tool-and-provider-strategies)
Tool and provider strategies
In v1, there are two strategies:
  * `toolStrategy` uses artificial tool calling to generate structured output
  * `providerStrategy` uses provider-native structured output generation


v1 (new)
v0 (old)

```
import { createAgent, toolStrategy } from "langchain";
import * as z from "zod";

const OutputSchema = z.object({
  summary: z.string(),
  sentiment: z.string(),
});

const agent = createAgent({
  model: "gpt-4.1-mini",
  tools,
  // explicitly using tool strategy
  responseFormat: toolStrategy(OutputSchema),
});

```

#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#prompted-output-removed)
Prompted output removed
Prompted output via custom instructions in `responseFormat` is removed in favor of the above strategies.
### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#streaming-node-name-rename)
Streaming node name rename
When streaming events from agents, the node name was changed from `"agent"` to `"model"` to better reflect the node’s purpose.
### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#runtime-context)
Runtime context
When invoking an agent, pass static, read-only configuration via the `context` config argument. This replaces patterns that used `config.configurable`.
v1 (new)
v0 (old)

```
import { createAgent, HumanMessage } from "langchain";
import * as z from "zod";

const agent = createAgent({
  model: "gpt-4.1",
  tools,
  contextSchema: z.object({ userId: z.string(), sessionId: z.string() }),
});

const result = await agent.invoke(
  { messages: [new HumanMessage("Hello")] },
  { context: { userId: "123", sessionId: "abc" } },
);

```

The old `config.configurable` pattern still works for backward compatibility, but using the new `context` parameter is recommended for new applications or applications migrating to v1.
* * *
## 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#standard-content)
Standard content
In v1, messages gain provider-agnostic standard content blocks. Access them via `message.contentBlocks` for a consistent, typed view across providers. The existing `message.content` field remains unchanged for strings or provider-native structures.
### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#what-changed)
What changed
  * New `contentBlocks` property on messages for normalized content.
  * New TypeScript types under `ContentBlock` for strong typing.
  * Optional serialization of standard blocks into `content` via `LC_OUTPUT_VERSION=v1` or `outputVersion: "v1"`.


### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#read-standardized-content)
Read standardized content
v1 (new)
v0 (old)

```
import { initChatModel } from "langchain";

const model = await initChatModel("gpt-5-nano");
const response = await model.invoke("Explain AI");

for (const block of response.contentBlocks) {
  if (block.type === "reasoning") {
    console.log(block.reasoning);
  } else if (block.type === "text") {
    console.log(block.text);
  }
}

```

### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#create-multimodal-messages)
Create multimodal messages
v1 (new)
v0 (old)

```
import { HumanMessage } from "langchain";

const message = new HumanMessage({
  contentBlocks: [
    { type: "text", text: "Describe this image." },
    { type: "image", url: "https://example.com/image.jpg" },
  ],
});
const res = await model.invoke([message]);

```

### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#example-block-types)
Example block types

```
import { ContentBlock } from "langchain";

const textBlock: ContentBlock.Text = {
  type: "text",
  text: "Hello world",
};

const imageBlock: ContentBlock.Multimodal.Image = {
  type: "image",
  url: "https://example.com/image.png",
  mimeType: "image/png",
};

```

See the content blocks [reference](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langchain/messages#content-block-reference) for more details.
### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#serialize-standard-content)
Serialize standard content
Standard content blocks are **not serialized** into the `content` attribute by default. If you need to access standard content blocks in the `content` attribute (e.g., when sending messages to a client), you can opt-in to serializing them into `content`.

```
export LC_OUTPUT_VERSION=v1

```

Learn more: [Messages](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langchain/messages#message-content) and [Standard content blocks](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langchain/messages#standard-content-blocks). See [Multimodal](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langchain/messages#multimodal) for input examples.
* * *
## 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#simplified-package)
Simplified package
The `langchain` package namespace is streamlined to focus on agent building blocks. Legacy functionality has moved to `@langchain/classic`. The new package exposes only the most useful and relevant functionality.
### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#exports)
Exports
The v1 package includes:  
| Module  | What’s available  | Notes  |  
| --- | --- | --- |  
| Agents  |  `createAgent`, `AgentState`  | Core agent creation functionality  |  
| Messages  | Message types, content blocks, `trimMessages`  | Re-exported from `@langchain/core`  |  
| Tools  |  `tool`, tool classes  | Re-exported from `@langchain/core`  |  
| Chat models  |  `initChatModel`, `BaseChatModel`  | Unified model initialization  |  
### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#@langchain/classic)
`@langchain/classic`
If you use legacy chains, the indexing API, or functionality previously re-exported from `@langchain/community`, install `@langchain/classic` and update imports:
npm
pnpm
yarn
bun

```
npm install @langchain/classic

```


```
// v1 (new)
import { ... } from "@langchain/classic";
import { ... } from "@langchain/classic/chains";

// v0 (old)
import { ... } from "langchain";
import { ... } from "langchain/chains";

```

* * *
## 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#breaking-changes)
Breaking changes
### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#dropped-node-18-support)
Dropped Node 18 support
All LangChain packages now require **Node.js 20 or higher**. Node.js 18 reached [end of life](https://nodejs.org/en/about/releases/) in March 2025.
### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#new-build-outputs)
New build outputs
Builds for all langchain packages now use a bundler based approach instead of using raw typescript outputs. If you were importing files from the `dist/` directory (which is not recommended), you will need to update your imports to use the new module system.
### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#legacy-code-moved-to-@langchain/classic)
Legacy code moved to `@langchain/classic`
Legacy functionality outside the focus of standard interfaces and agents has been moved to the [`@langchain/classic`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@langchain/classic) package. See the [Simplified package](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#simplified-package) section for details on what’s available in the core `langchain` package and what moved to `@langchain/classic`.
### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#removal-of-deprecated-apis)
Removal of deprecated APIs
Methods, functions, and other objects that were already deprecated and slated for removal in 1.0 have been deleted.
View removed deprecated APIs
The following deprecated APIs have been removed in v1:
#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#core-functionality)
Core functionality
  * `TraceGroup` - Use LangSmith tracing instead
  * `BaseDocumentLoader.loadAndSplit` - Use `.load()` followed by a text splitter
  * `RemoteRunnable` - No longer supported


#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#prompts-2)
Prompts
  * `BasePromptTemplate.serialize` and `.deserialize` - Use JSON serialization directly
  * `ChatPromptTemplate.fromPromptMessages` - Use `ChatPromptTemplate.fromMessages`


#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#retrievers)
Retrievers
  * `BaseRetrieverInterface.getRelevantDocuments` - Use `.invoke()` instead


#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#runnables)
Runnables
  * `Runnable.bind` - Use `.bindTools()` or other specific binding methods
  * `Runnable.map` - Use `.batch()` instead
  * `RunnableBatchOptions.maxConcurrency` - Use `maxConcurrency` in the config object


#### 
[​](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/migrate/langchain-v1#chat-models)
Chat models
